  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Post election violence - Kakamega</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kakamega district is one of the eight districts in Western Province, situated in the Western part of Kenya. The area contains the Kakamega forest and is mainly flat; receiving a very high amount of annual rain and. The local inhabitant&#x2019;s main economic activity is subsistence farming and fishing on their own land or hired land. The population of the district is approximately 2.5 million people from different ethnic groups with the majority coming from the Luhya community. 

Tension started just before the post election results were announced whereby there was widespread suspicion and false allegations that the settlers had illegally occupied the land given to them when the white settlers left after independence in 1963.

What followed was much violence, destruction and displacement of people from ethnic communities other than the Luhya community and the formation of internally displaced camps in several sites in the district.

Most of the affected families were from Likuyani and Mautuma, which are settlement schemes where people from different communities bought land years ago from the white settlers and practice farming. 

To date, families at Likuyani and Mautuma have returned to their land/ places of displacement, but because they spent most of last year (2008) in displaced camps, approximately 65% of the displaced people have not cultivated their lands and currently lying fallow.

Business persons who were operating in Kakamega town and other smaller towns have lost their business capital and now living on relief support from families and relatives. Some business persons are engaged in petty trade as hawkers in Kakamega town. 

This project does not use funds from CAFOD, as it is funded by the Irish government through Trocaire and part of a Post Election Violence consortia between CAFOD and Trocaire.</narrative>
  </description>
